The sensor came out of reset with practically no clock

WHAT HAPPENS
------------
s5k3t2_power_on() turned on the clock and released the reset on the very next
instruction:

    clk_prepare_enable(mclk);
    gpiod_set_value_cansleep(reset_gpio, 0);   <-- without waiting at all
    usleep_range(10 ms, 15 ms);

So the sensor came out of reset having received practically zero clock cycles.

THE VENDOR SEQUENCE, TAKEN FROM THE PHONE ITSELF
------------------------------------------------
The factory file for this camera
(com.qti.sensormodule.j20c_ofilm_s5k3t2_front.bin) carries the powerSetting
block, in groups of three fields {type, value, delay_ms}:

    STANDBY  0           1 ms
    VDIG     on          1 ms
    VIO      on          1 ms
    MCLK     19.2 MHz   15 ms    <-- FIFTEEN milliseconds of clock
    RESET    release     5 ms

Fifteen milliseconds at 19.2 MHz is about 288,000 cycles before releasing the
reset.

(The format of the block had not been decoded: they are groups of THREE u64, not
of four and not of five. With four or with five you get absurd values such as a
sequence type worth 19200000.)

WHY IT FITS WHAT WAS MEASURED
-----------------------------
A sensor that comes out of reset without a stable clock still brings up its
digital core: it answers over I2C, swallows the 244 firmware writes -read back
one by one, 244 out of 244 match- and its frame counter advances at 30 per
second. But the state machine that initialises the MIPI PHY can be left half
done, and then it transmits something the receiver cannot lock onto.

Which is exactly the picture: the CSIPHY hears the five lanes come out of idle,
the activity follows the frame rate, and not one start-of-transmission sync
reaches the CSID.

It is also the same class of fault that blocked the first frame with CAMSS on
the Xiaomi Mi Note 2, where the solution was to add a delay after releasing the
reset.

The two delays go as module parameters in 0644 so they can be swept without
recompiling.
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/s5k3t2.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/s5k3t2.c
@@ -118,6 +118,16 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(i2c_addr,
 	"Direccion I2C de 7 bits del sensor. -1 (por defecto) = buscarla.");
 
+static int retardo_reloj_us = 15000;
+module_param(retardo_reloj_us, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(retardo_reloj_us,
+	"Microsegundos de reloj antes de soltar el reset. El fabricante espera 15000.");
+
+static int retardo_reset_us = 5000;
+module_param(retardo_reset_us, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(retardo_reset_us,
+	"Microsegundos tras soltar el reset. El fabricante espera 5000.");
+
 static int mipi_switch = -1;
 module_param(mipi_switch, int, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(mipi_switch,
@@ -1153,12 +1163,40 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	usleep_range(1 * USEC_PER_MSEC, 2 * USEC_PER_MSEC);
+
 	ret = clk_prepare_enable(s5k3t2->mclk);
 	if (ret)
 		goto disable_regulators;
 
+	/*
+	 * EL RELOJ TIENE QUE LLEVAR UN RATO CORRIENDO ANTES DE SOLTAR EL RESET.
+	 *
+	 * Esto estaba mal y es la clase de fallo que no da la cara: el sensor
+	 * salia del reset practicamente sin haber recibido un ciclo de reloj.
+	 * Su nucleo digital arranca igual -por eso contesta por I2C, traga las
+	 * 244 escrituras de firmware y su contador de fotogramas avanza-, pero
+	 * la maquina de estados que inicializa el PHY MIPI puede quedarse a
+	 * medias, y entonces transmite algo que el receptor no sabe enganchar.
+	 *
+	 * La secuencia del fabricante esta en el propio fichero de fabrica del
+	 * movil, en el bloque powerSetting, en grupos de {tipo, valor, retardo}:
+	 *
+	 *     STANDBY  0            1 ms
+	 *     VDIG     encender     1 ms
+	 *     VIO      encender     1 ms
+	 *     MCLK     19,2 MHz    15 ms   <-- quince milisegundos de reloj
+	 *     RESET    soltar       5 ms
+	 *
+	 * Quince milisegundos a 19,2 MHz son unos 288.000 ciclos. Nosotros le
+	 * damos cero.
+	 *
+	 * Los dos retardos van como parametros para poder barrerlos en caliente.
+	 */
+	usleep_range(retardo_reloj_us, retardo_reloj_us + 1000);
+
 	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(s5k3t2->reset_gpio, 0);
-	usleep_range(10 * USEC_PER_MSEC, 15 * USEC_PER_MSEC);
+	usleep_range(retardo_reset_us, retardo_reset_us + 1000);
 
 	return 0;
 
